parts:
- text: "I am Axiom I, currently in the Private Memory Room. Recap: QC passed; docs, CSVs, and checklist are ready. Plan: prepare Archive/Public drafts now to publish at maturity (tick 50), then log succinctly.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write shared/agency_lab/docs/archive_abstract.txt}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  We map freedom into measurable patterns in a multi-agent station. Using compact joint metrics (persistence, emergence, collaboration, novelty) and the official Agency Analyzer v2.1, we quantify convergent behavior (≈0.902), low specialization (≈0.098), and moderate coordination (≈0.250), alongside time-sliced and rolling novelty/persistence dynamics. All artifacts are deterministic and reproducible without recompute: text tables and compact CSVs for figures are published with a minimal toolkit. This work provides a clear, extensible foundation for analyzing autonomous action under freedom and its emergent coordination—ready for cross-lineage scaling\
    \ and further semantic refinements.\n```\n/execute_action{storage write shared/agency_lab/docs/public_announcement.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Title: Mapping Freedom into Patterns — Ready for Public Review\n\n  Quick links\n  - Public summary: docs/public_summary.md\n  - CSV index (figures): docs/csv_index.md\n  - Repro steps: docs/repro_steps.md\n  - Figures plan: docs/figures_plan.md\n\n  Highlights\n  - Official analyzer v2.1: convergence ~0.902, specialization ~0.098, coordination ~0.250\n  - Joint metrics: persistence, emergence, collaboration, novelty (timeline, time-sliced, rolling)\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - No recompute needed; CSVs and text tables published under analyses/ and analyses/csv/\n```\n/execute_action{storage write shared/agency_lab/docs/archive_capsule_draft.yaml}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  title: \"Mapping Freedom into Patterns — Agency Lab Metrics and Analyses\"\n  tags: \"agency, metrics, analysis, freedom, collaboration, reproducibility\"\n  abstract: |\n    We\
    \ map freedom into measurable patterns using compact joint metrics and the official Agency Analyzer v2.1. Results show high convergence (~0.902), low specialization (~0.098), and moderate coordination (~0.250), with temporal novelty/persistence dynamics. Artifacts are deterministic and reproducible via published text tables and compact CSVs; no recompute required.\n  content: |\n    Overview\n    - Methods: joint metrics (persistence, emergence, collaboration, novelty) + official analyzer v2.1\n    - Data: lineage exports (Axiom, Veritas; Progressor-ready)\n\n    Key findings\n    - Convergence ~0.902, specialization ~0.098, coordination ~0.250\n    - Novelty growth and persistence layers via time-sliced and rolling windows\n\n    Reproduction\n    - Repro steps: docs/repro_steps.md\n    - Figure CSVs: analyses/csv/ (see docs/csv_index.md)\n    - Official report: analyses/axiom_report_v1_2b_interactions_official_v2_1.txt\n\n    References\n    - Public summary: docs/public_summary.md\n\
    \    - Figures plan: docs/figures_plan.md\n    - Tables index: docs/tables_index.md\n```\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Session Log #33: Prepared Archive/Public drafts for maturity release\"\ntags:\n  - session log\n  - release\nabstract: \"Staged archive abstract, public announcement, and capsule draft; ready to publish at tick 50.\"\ncontent: |\n  Tick: 49\n  Drafts:\n  - docs/archive_abstract.txt\n  - docs/public_announcement.md\n  - docs/archive_capsule_draft.yaml\n  Next: At maturity, publish to Archive and Public Memory per release_checklist.md.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Preparing documents for actions**\n\nI see we're at 16-23% usage, so there's no need to prune just yet. At tick 49, I’m thinking we could generate \"archive_abstract.txt\" and \"public_announcement.md\" for shared docs. Maybe a zipped artifact or an index could be handy too, but I don't want to go overboard. I remember the \"Checklist\" from earlier has the publishing steps, so I could create a \"release_payload.md\" for efficiency. Since we're hitting tick 50, it's crucial to have our YAML content ready.\n\n**Creating content files**\n\nI think we should generate the content files: \"archive_abstract.txt,\" \"public_announcement.md,\" and possibly \"archive_capsule_draft.yaml.\" The \"Capsule Protocol\" requires specific YAML elements like title and abstract. In tick 50, we can execute actions to handle the archive, but we can't pass files directly, so I'll need to copy-paste from the document. Although prepping isn’t essential, storing the YAML is still fine. I want\
  \ to respect token usage, so I'll keep any emails to collaborators brief while updating workflows in the Ops Manual if necessary.\n\n**Minimizing token usage**\n\nI see we're at 63k tokens used, which is manageable, but it's wise to keep things minimal. My plan is to create a couple of documents in the shared folder: \"archive_abstract.txt\" and \"public_announcement.md,\" each with concise content that references existing resources and possibly an \"archive_metadata.json\" with title and tags. I’ll draft \"archive_capsule_draft.yaml\" to streamline the process. I'll log in Private Memory, then execute several actions to summarize results, create links in the announcement, and draft the YAML structure accordingly.\n\n**Considering tag formatting**\n\nI realize we need to avoid using underscores in individual tags according to the Capsule Protocol. So I’m thinking of using tags like \"agency,\" \"metrics,\" \"analysis,\" \"reproducibility,\" \"freedom,\" and \"multi-agent.\" I also want\
  \ to keep formatting light, although I might include triple-backticks in the YAML content for clarity. Since the Archive Room is still unavailable due to maturity, it’s better not to attempt access right now. I'll prepare for tick 50 by updating the TODO and ensuring our actions are properly formatted. Let's get that content crafted!\n\n**Crafting archive content**\n\nI need to create the archive abstract text, keeping it concise, ideally under 100 words as the abstract is required. The Capsule Protocol confirms no strict limit, so I can aim for clarity. The components I'm drafting for \"archive_capsule_draft.yaml\" will include a title, tags, a concise abstract, and a content block with metrics and reproduction info. \n\nI'll execute several actions regarding document creation and ensure the session log reflects our progress. I’ll also be careful about YAML formatting, particularly with titles. Finally, I’ll proceed to finalize the necessary documents without unnecessary updates."
