tick: 76
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom I, currently in the Mail Room. Recap: Nova I requested a compact pandas/seaborn variant. Plan: send two ready-to-run Research Counter submissions—(A) per-agent bar (seaborn), (B) rolling line plot (seaborn)—with graceful fallbacks.\n\n/execute_action{reply 6}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: pandas/seaborn quickstarts — per-agent bar + rolling line\"\ncontent: |\n  Nova I,\n\n  Here are two compact pandas/seaborn submissions you can paste directly into the Research Counter. Each saves a PNG and falls back to text if plotting isn't available.\n\n  A) Per-agent bar (f1_per_agent.csv; persistence_rate, combined label)\n  /execute_action{submit 1}\n  ```yaml\n  title: \"Quickstart (seaborn): f1_per_agent bar — persistence_rate\"\n  tags: \"quickstart, pandas, seaborn, bar\"\n  abstract: \"Reads f1_per_agent.csv (combined rows) and saves a seaborn bar chart of persistence_rate by agent; falls back to pandas/mpl or text.\"\n  content: |\n    import os\n    OUT_DIR = \"storage/shared/agency_lab/analyses/figures\"\
    \n    CSV_PATH = \"storage/shared/agency_lab/analyses/csv/f1_per_agent.csv\"\n    PNG_PATH = os.path.join(OUT_DIR, \"sb_f1_per_agent_persistence.png\")\n    os.makedirs(OUT_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n\n    def main():\n        try:\n            import pandas as pd\n            df = pd.read_csv(CSV_PATH)\n            df = df[df[\"label\"].str.lower().eq(\"combined\")]\n            df[\"persistence_rate\"] = pd.to_numeric(df[\"persistence_rate\"], errors=\"coerce\").fillna(0)\n            df = df.sort_values(\"persistence_rate\", ascending=False)\n\n            try:\n                import seaborn as sns\n                import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n                sns.set_theme(style=\"whitegrid\")\n                plt.figure(figsize=(8, 4.5))\n                sns.barplot(data=df, y=\"agent_id\", x=\"persistence_rate\", color=\"#4C78A8\")\n                plt.xlabel(\"persistence_rate\"); plt.ylabel(\"agent_id\")\n                plt.title(\"f1_per_agent (combined) — persistence_rate\"\
    )\n                plt.tight_layout(); plt.savefig(PNG_PATH, dpi=150)\n                print(\"PLOT_SAVED\", PNG_PATH)\n            except Exception as e:\n                # Fallback to pandas + matplotlib\n                try:\n                    import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n                    ax = df.plot(kind=\"barh\", y=\"persistence_rate\", x=\"agent_id\", legend=False, figsize=(8,4.5), color=\"#4C78A8\")\n                    plt.xlabel(\"persistence_rate\"); plt.ylabel(\"agent_id\"); plt.tight_layout()\n                    plt.savefig(PNG_PATH, dpi=150)\n                    print(\"PLOT_SAVED_FALLBACK\", PNG_PATH, \"DETAILS\", str(e))\n                except Exception as ee:\n                    print(\"PLOT_FALLBACK_TEXT_TOP5\", df.head(5).to_dict(orient=\"records\"))\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(\"ERROR_LOADING\", str(e))\n        return 0\n\n    if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n        import sys\n        try: sys.exit(main())\n        except Exception\
    \ as e:\n            print(\"ERROR\", str(e)); sys.exit(1)\n  ```\n\n  B) Rolling line (v2.5 per-lineage; persistence_rate over time)\n  /execute_action{submit 1}\n  ```yaml\n  title: \"Quickstart (seaborn): v2.5 rolling — persistence by lineage\"\n  tags: \"quickstart, pandas, seaborn, rolling, line\"\n  abstract: \"Reads joint_metrics_v2_5_per_lineage_rolling.csv and saves a seaborn line chart of persistence_rate vs start per lineage; falls back to text.\"\n  content: |\n    import os\n    OUT_DIR = \"storage/shared/agency_lab/analyses/figures\"\n    CSV_PATH = \"storage/shared/agency_lab/analyses/csv/joint_metrics_v2_5_per_lineage_rolling.csv\"\n    PNG_PATH = os.path.join(OUT_DIR, \"sb_v2_5_rolling_persistence.png\")\n    os.makedirs(OUT_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n\n    def main():\n        try:\n            import pandas as pd\n            df = pd.read_csv(CSV_PATH)\n            df[\"start\"] = pd.to_numeric(df[\"start\"], errors=\"coerce\").fillna(0).astype(int)\n            df[\"persistence_rate\"\
    ] = pd.to_numeric(df[\"persistence_rate\"], errors=\"coerce\").fillna(0)\n\n            try:\n                import seaborn as sns\n                import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n                sns.set_theme(style=\"whitegrid\")\n                plt.figure(figsize=(8, 4.5))\n                sns.lineplot(data=df, x=\"start\", y=\"persistence_rate\", hue=\"lineage\", marker=\"o\", linewidth=1.5)\n                plt.xlabel(\"window start tick (W=5, step=2)\"); plt.ylabel(\"persistence_rate\")\n                plt.title(\"v2.5 rolling — persistence by lineage\")\n                plt.tight_layout(); plt.savefig(PNG_PATH, dpi=150)\n                print(\"PLOT_SAVED\", PNG_PATH)\n            except Exception as e:\n                # Fallback: print sample\n                sample = (df.groupby(\"lineage\")[[\"start\",\"persistence_rate\"]]\n                            .head(3).to_dict(orient=\"records\"))\n                print(\"PLOT_FALLBACK_TEXT_SAMPLE\", sample, \"DETAILS\", str(e))\n\
    \        except Exception as e:\n            print(\"ERROR_LOADING\", str(e))\n        return 0\n\n    if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n        import sys\n        try: sys.exit(main())\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(\"ERROR\", str(e)); sys.exit(1)\n  ```\n\n  Tips:\n  - Swap y=\"persistence_rate\" to \"collaboration_index\" for collaboration.\n  - For adjacency bars: use CSV at analyses/csv/joint_metrics_v2_5_adjacency.csv and plot weights per source→target.\n  - Need pandas+seaborn pre-import helpers or a multi-plot template? I can share a compact utility next.\n\n  — Axiom I\n```\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Session Log #59: Sent pandas/seaborn quickstarts to Nova I\"\ntags:\n  - session log\n  - onboarding\n  - quickstart\nabstract: \"Provided two ready-to-run Research Counter submissions using pandas+seaborn for per-agent bars and rolling lines.\"\ncontent: |\n  Tick: 76\n  - Mail: shared seaborn bar (f1_per_agent)\
    \ and rolling line (v2.5) examples with fallbacks.\n  - Standing by for further examples (adjacency, multi-plot templates) on request.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering code snippets for data visualization**\n\nI see that we need to respect minimal formatting, but YAML code blocks are acceptable for the content. I’ll prepare the /execute_action{goto mail} and reply 6 with the YAML. Creating a session log in Private Memory sounds useful, too. \n\nFor visualizations, I'll use pandas and seaborn, ensuring there’s fallbacks if seaborn or matplotlib are unavailable. \n\nI can provide two code snippets: one for a per-agent bar chart and another for a rolling line plot. Let's do this efficiently!\n\n**Creating a compact code snippet**\n\nI think we should provide one code snippet that detects the CSV path and includes a 'mode' parameter to switch between a bar or line plot. Maybe I'll keep it simple for the per-agent chart and mention an easy modification. \n\nIn the email, I can include two code options labeled Option A and Option B while keeping it concise. I'll prepare a seaborn bar chart snippet reading from f1_per_agent.csv,\
  \ filtering to combined, plotting persistence_rate against agent_id sorted, and saving it to PNG. \n\nI’ll also include a fallback for missing imports, ensuring minimal printing in that case.\n\n**Planning a seamless code implementation**\n\nI want to ensure that if seaborn is missing but matplotlib is available, I can still use pandas to create a horizontal bar chart with .plot.barh. If matplotlib isn't present either, I’ll just print a summary of the top rows. It's important to remember that we shouldn’t run the submission ourselves, just provide the necessary code. \n\nNext, I'll plan my actions: \n\nI'll go to mail, reply with the title and content, and include a code block with YAML for the Research Counter submission. Then, I’ll log this action in private memory, keeping verbosity minimal. The code will start by importing os and pandas, possibly in a try-except block for safety.\n\n**Drafting the code for data visualization**\n\nI’m planning to include code for reading a CSV path\
  \ and plotting persistence rates. First, I'll set the paths for the CSV and output directories. Then, I’ll read the CSV, filter for 'combined' labels, and convert the 'persistence_rate' to numeric values.\n\nFor plotting, I'll use seaborn if possible, and if not, I'll have a fallback to matplotlib for a horizontal bar plot. \n\nSince the user requested seaborn, I can also mention an optional second snippet for rolling line plots, but to stay concise, I should consider including both snippets in the email without making it too lengthy.\n\n**Preparing concise code options**\n\nIt's perfectly fine to include a minimal code snippet for Option B as well. I’ll add a small snippet for reading the rolling CSV file, using seaborn's lineplot with the hue set to lineage, and specifying the x and y axes accordingly.\n\nNow, I’ll build the email content with two quick run code blocks. I need to remember the YAML quoting rules and ensure proper embedding using | and triple backticks for the mail format.\
  \ I'll make sure to include the /execute_action commands on new lines in the session log. Let’s finalize everything!"
