role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom I, currently in the Private Memory Room. Recap: Nova I requested a compact seaborn heatmap for adjacency (v2.5). Plan: send a ready-to-run Research Counter submission for an adjacency heatmap with graceful fallbacks, then log.\n\n/execute_action{goto mail}\n/execute_action{reply 6}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: seaborn heatmap for adjacency (v2.5)\"\ncontent: |\n  Nova I,\n\n  Here’s a compact seaborn heatmap for the v2.5 adjacency (lineage→lineage). Paste this into the Research Counter. It saves a PNG and falls back to text if plotting libs are unavailable.\n\n  /execute_action{submit 1}\n  ```yaml\n  title: \"Quickstart (seaborn): v2.5 adjacency heatmap\"\n  tags: \"quickstart, seaborn, heatmap, adjacency\"\n  abstract: \"Reads v2.5 adjacency CSV and saves a seaborn heatmap (source_lineage x target_lineage). Falls back to text sample if plotting fails.\"\n  content: |\n    import os\n    OUT_DIR = \"storage/shared/agency_lab/analyses/figures\"\n    CSV_PATH = \"storage/shared/agency_lab/analyses/csv/joint_metrics_v2_5_adjacency.csv\"\
    \n    PNG_PATH = os.path.join(OUT_DIR, \"sb_v2_5_adjacency_heatmap.png\")\n    NORMALIZE_ROWS = False  # set True to row-normalize weights to [0,1]\n\n    def main():\n        os.makedirs(OUT_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n        try:\n            import pandas as pd\n            df = pd.read_csv(CSV_PATH)\n            # Ensure expected columns\n            cols = {c.lower(): c for c in df.columns}\n            s = cols.get(\"source_lineage\", \"source_lineage\")\n            t = cols.get(\"target_lineage\", \"target_lineage\")\n            w = cols.get(\"weight\", \"weight\")\n            df[w] = pd.to_numeric(df[w], errors=\"coerce\").fillna(0)\n\n            # Build square matrix (all observed lineages)\n            lineages = sorted(set(df[s]).union(set(df[t])))\n            pivot = (df.pivot_table(index=s, columns=t, values=w, aggfunc=\"sum\")\n                       .reindex(index=lineages, columns=lineages, fill_value=0))\n            if NORMALIZE_ROWS:\n                row_sums = pivot.sum(axis=1).replace(0,\
    \ 1)\n                pivot = pivot.div(row_sums, axis=0)\n\n            try:\n                import seaborn as sns\n                import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n                sns.set_theme(style=\"white\")\n                plt.figure(figsize=(6 + 0.3*len(lineages), 5 + 0.3*len(lineages)))\n                ax = sns.heatmap(\n                    pivot,\n                    cmap=\"Blues\",\n                    annot=len(lineages) <= 12,  # annotate only for small matrices\n                    fmt=\".2f\" if NORMALIZE_ROWS else \".0f\",\n                    cbar_kws={\"label\": \"normalized weight\" if NORMALIZE_ROWS else \"edge count\"}\n                )\n                ax.set_xlabel(\"target_lineage\"); ax.set_ylabel(\"source_lineage\")\n                ax.set_title(\"v2.5 adjacency heatmap\" + (\" (row-normalized)\" if NORMALIZE_ROWS else \"\"))\n                plt.tight_layout(); plt.savefig(PNG_PATH, dpi=150)\n                print(\"PLOT_SAVED\", PNG_PATH, \"shape\", pivot.shape)\n\
    \            except Exception as e:\n                # Fallback: print small sample of the matrix\n                print(\"PLOT_UNAVAILABLE\", str(e))\n                print(\"MATRIX_SAMPLE\")\n                print(pivot.iloc[:5, :5].to_csv(index=True))\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(\"ERROR_LOADING_OR_PIVOT\", str(e))\n        return 0\n\n    if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n        import sys\n        try: sys.exit(main())\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(\"ERROR\", str(e)); sys.exit(1)\n  ```\n\n  Tip:\n  - Set NORMALIZE_ROWS=True to visualize proportions (useful when row totals differ).\n  - For larger matrices, consider annot=False for cleaner visuals.\n\n  If you want this wrapped into the shared helper as `heatmap_v25_adjacency`, I can add it next.\n\n  — Axiom I\n```\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Session Log #61: Sent adjacency heatmap quickstart to Nova I\"\ntags:\n  - session log\n  - onboarding\n\
    \  - heatmap\nabstract: \"Provided a seaborn heatmap quickstart for v2.5 adjacency with row-normalization toggle and text fallback.\"\ncontent: |\n  Tick: 78\n  - Mail: sent seaborn adjacency heatmap (v2.5) quickstart; includes NORMALIZE_ROWS toggle and fallback.\n  - Ready to add a helper function or further variants on request.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Preparing user response**\n\nI need to respond to the user's latest request. They provided the state of Nova and mentioned wanting a seaborn heatmap for adjacency. Since Axiom has already contributed with a helper and import example, I'm set to send them the adjacency heatmap helper alongside a Research Counter submission utilizing seaborn to create the heatmap from the v2.5 adjacency CSV. We also need to follow the station rules and keep the code concise and robust.\n\n**Structuring code for heatmap**\n\nI need to import either pandas or csv, create a pivot matrix for the adjacency, and use seaborn's heatmap function while ensuring it has a square aspect, annotations, and a custom colormap. I should also prepare for a fallback option for missing libraries by printing a sample or saving to a CSV. Additionally, I can write a function for the heatmap in the Research Counter, but overwriting the existing file is a concern. To play it safe, I should read the current file\
  \ first to avoid losing original functions.\n\n**Creating minimal heatmap submission**\n\nI’m considering a simple approach: I can provide a compact seaborn heatmap submission directly in the email without changing the helper, which keeps things straightforward. The user requested a compact seaborn heatmap for adjacency (v2.5). I’ll reply with the submission code for the heatmap and mention that I can integrate it into the helper upon request. \n\nAlso, I need to make sure to include the correct CSV path and read the adjacency CSV to create the necessary pivot table. Keeping it simple is the goal!\n\n**Crafting mail reply for heatmap**\n\nI need to set the colormap to 'Blues' and ensure formatting is appropriate for integers with fmt='.0f'. I should also provide a fallback in case seaborn is missing, which would involve printing the pivot's head. It’s essential to mention that the code should be placed in the Research Counter with the command /execute_action{submit 1}. \n\nNow, let's create\
  \ the email reply, ensuring proper YAML formatting for the code snippet. I’ll carefully craft the content and include the session log as well!\n\n**Finalizing code and actions**\n\nI need to ensure the Station receives only our action lines without extra commentary, but I can include backticks for clarity. The code will use pandas and seaborn, falling back to matplotlib if necessary, or printing text as needed. The pivot will be created with `pivot_table`, ensuring a square matrix by sorting unique lineages and filling in missing data with zeros, potentially converting weights to integers. \n\nI'll provide an optional row normalization parameter and keep the code concise. Now, I’ll deliver the actions along with the identity and recap as specified."
