  \ explicitly \"sleep\" or \"poll,\" but historically, the \"Running experiments\" and \"Submitted Evaluations\" sections update after submission.\n\n**Considering storage read strategy**\n\nI’m thinking about attempting to read the expected CSV files from storage, but there’s really no risk since if they’re missing, I’d just get a \"File not found\" response—though that would be noise I want to avoid. Since I need to conserve tokens, it’s better to fix the tags, resubmit, and inform the progressor about the previous submission failure. I can mention the expected output paths and log that in Private Memory. I should also plan for the next prune after 4 ticks from tick 92, which is now at tick 98, meaning I might be able to do that after 6 more ticks.\n\n**Planning token pruning**\n\nI’ve been considering pruning ticks 83 to 90 now to keep my token usage lean, especially since it’s currently at a comfortable 55k tokens. It seems wise to execute the pruning even though it was scheduled for\
  \ tick 97. The Token Management Room still shows many ticks in the \"Dialogue History,\" but I’ve already pruned some previous blocks. Since the ticks 83 to 90 aren’t protected, I can prune them together. I’ll summarize the relevant items but make sure not to overdo conciseness, including important details like the \"public_4 reply\" and the creation of the trust adapter doc at tick 94.\n\n**Finalizing pruning and submission actions**\n\nLooks like we need to prune ticks 83 to 90, which include some logs, but I can provide a comprehensive summary to keep context. I’ll submit the evaluation with the adjusted tags: \"adapter, trust, csv, rolling, adjacency,\" ensuring no underscores in the tags. I need to compose a brief email to the progressor to let them know about the resubmission and the new evaluation paths. \n\nAfter that, I’ll execute the pruning actions and ensure to follow the important YAML formatting rules for titles and summaries. I'll keep everything concise and clear, avoiding\
  \ unnecessary details. Let’s get this done!"
