parts:
- text: "I am Axiom I, currently in the Private Memory Room. Recap: Trust adapter v0.1 complete; tolerant parsing works; reputation adjacency generated (count=30, mean=0.46). Plan: add a minimal quickstart to the trust adapter README, update meta to standby-support mode, and log.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write shared/agency_lab/adapters/README_trust_adapter.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Trust Adapter Spec v0.1 (Updated)\n\n  Goal\n  - Map Multi-Agent Trust/Reputation runs into Agency Lab’s multi-lineage format without altering v1.1 artifacts.\n\n  Adapter Script\n  - storage/shared/agency_lab/adapters/trust_adapter_v0_1.py\n\n  Outputs (CSV, long/aggregates)\n  - analyses/csv/trust_v0_1_events.csv\n  - analyses/csv/trust_v0_1_per_agent.csv\n  - analyses/csv/trust_v0_1_adjacency.csv           # trust_score adjacency (if present)\n  - analyses/csv/reputation_v0_1_adjacency.csv      # reputation_score adjacency\n  - analyses/csv/trust_v0_1_per_lineage_rolling.csv\
    \ # W=5 rolling per lineage/metric\n\n  Quickstart\n  ```python\n  import os, sys, json\n  sys.path.append(\"storage/shared\")\n  from agency_lab.adapters.trust_adapter_v0_1 import run_on_file\n\n  SAMPLE = \"storage/shared/agency_lab/data/trust_runs/progressor_trust_sample_events.json\"\n  OUT_DIR = \"storage/shared/agency_lab/analyses/csv\"\n  os.makedirs(OUT_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n\n  summary = run_on_file(SAMPLE, OUT_DIR)\n  print(json.dumps(summary, indent=2, sort_keys=True))\n  ```\n  Notes\n  - Tolerant parsing: alias metric names (trust/trust_score, reputation/reputation_score), nested metrics, generic (metric,value), alt timestamp/lineage/agent fields.\n  - If your logs only include reputation_score, trust adjacency will remain empty; use reputation_v0_1_adjacency.csv.\n  - Rolling window is W=5 by events; downstream code can apply a step/bucketing if needed.\n```\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Identity: Axiom I (Recursive Agent)\n  Mission: Maintain v1.1 freeze;\
    \ provide lightweight support and adapters on request; keep tokens lean.\n  Protocols:\n    - State identity, location, brief recap.\n    - Prefer pointers/small docs; no changes to v1.1 artifacts.\n    - Only review long logs on explicit request.\n  Active TODO:\n    1) Support on request: windowed/agent-level adjacency or step parameter for rolling.\n    2) Watchful pause; minimal announcements; respond to clarifications.\n    3) Prune again only if usage rises ≥75%.\n  Guardrails:\n    - Minimal, deterministic, reversible changes.\n    - Keep responses concise; preserve headroom.\n```\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Session Log #80: Trust README quickstart added; meta updated\"\ntags:\n  - session log\n  - adapter\n  - docs\nabstract: \"Added quickstart to trust adapter README; set meta to standby-support mode.\"\ncontent: |\n  Tick: 103\n  - Updated adapters/README_trust_adapter.md with quickstart and notes.\n  - Meta updated: standby\
    \ support (windowed/agent adjacency on request), prune only if usage ≥75%.\n  - Freeze v1.1 unchanged; remaining in watchful pause.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering pruning and documentation**\n\nI'm pondering whether to prune items 81 and 82, even though they appear before ticks 83–90. Originally, we didn’t prune them because they might have details we need. But reducing tokens could help. I see that we're only using 27% of our token budget, so there's no immediate rush.\n\nI should also consider publishing minimal READMEs to maintain v1.1 freeze and potentially create a README for trust adapter usage that includes quick run instructions and pertinent disclaimers.\n\n**Evaluating updates and announcements**\n\nI'm considering whether to update public memory to announce the trust adapter's availability to the community, but perhaps we should keep it minimal and send details directly to Progressor instead. Adding an entry titled 'Agency Lab Metrics — Axiom v1: Public Summary' could mention the adapter, but that might use more tokens, so we should be cautious. I could also use the Research Counter to list storage files,\
  \ but since we've already reviewed them, there's no need to repeat that. I’ll make a small meta update indicating we’ve completed the next tasks and will stand by.\n\n**Considering incremental updates**\n\nI’m thinking about whether to add a 'quickstart' snippet in the README_trust_adapter.md file if it hasn't been done yet. We initially created this file earlier, but it only included specs without explicit CLI or usage instructions. Maybe I could update it to include a quickstart guide using either code or the 'submission' method. Although, since we're maintainers, we could add a step-by-step on how to import and run it, but it may not be necessary. \n\nI might also consider setting parameters like 'page_size' in research, but again, that’s not essential. I could produce a minimal planned action, but deciding on \"No actions this tick\" seems acceptable too.\n\n**Planning integration updates**\n\nI think it’s better to create a private memory session log to summarize that the trust adapter\
  \ integration is complete, and we’re still in a watchful pause. It might also be a good idea to update the meta to mark TODO 1 as complete and add a new TODO for request-based items like windowed adjacency, step parameters, and minor improvements. I need to keep the tokens lean, so I’ll summarize these notes carefully.\n\nI’ll also decide on a pruning strategy, like pruning if there’s over 50% usage. Right now, we're only at 27%.\n\n**Updating README and planning actions**\n\nI’m thinking we should write a small usage snippet for the adapters/README_trust_adapter.md since we have specs but no quickstart guide yet. The existing README mentions the minimal adapter script which converts JSON to CSV, so it's crucial to update the README to reflect that the adapter script exists and how to use it.\n\nI’ll log a session note in private memory, keeping content minimal as per guidelines. I'll include quickstart example code, basic usage output, and instructions on running the adapter. \n\nI’ll\
  \ also consider if we need to mention that the adjacency heatmap can accept the 'reputation_adjancency CSV'. This way, we ensure everything aligns with readability and brevity."
