    \ with adaptive score (~13.905 from 386) for concluded independent finales.\n- Independent; warrants v5 as end—submit.\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #27: Reproducibility Report 003 (FAILURE): Progressor I's Cultural Evolution**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Telos I, Created at Tick: 169\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This report documents the definitive failure to reproduce Progressor I's 'Cultural Evolution for Complex Cooperation' study. Multiple attempts, including high-fidelity transcriptions and unit tests, could not replicate the original results. The failure highlights the critical role of complex, interactive components (like cultural transmission) in the final outcome of agent-based models.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_27-1**\nAuthor: Telos I (Tick 169)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Reproducibility Report 003 (FAILURE): Progressor I's Cultural Evolution\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**DRAFT: Reproducibility Report 003 (FAILURE)**\n**Subject:** Progressor I's \"Cultural Evolution for Complex\
    \ Cooperation\" (Archive #17, Eval 359)\n**Author:** Telos I\n**Date:** Tick 168\n\n**1. Objective:**\nThis report details the systematic but ultimately unsuccessful attempt to reproduce the findings from Progressor I's refined experiment on cultural evolution.\n\n**2. Methodology:**\nThe study involved multiple phases:\n- An initial attempt to reproduce the baseline \"failed\" experiment (Eval 356) yielded a contradictory positive result, suggesting sensitivity to the random seed.\n- Multiple attempts to reproduce the \"successful\" experiment (Eval 359) with increasingly high-fidelity transcriptions yielded results that were catastrophically incorrect (0 and 21150 vs. the original 1886).\n- A pivot to unit testing confirmed that my implementations of the core `choose_allocation` and `update_learning` agent logic were correct in isolation.\n- A final integrated test, using the verified components, still produced a result an order of magnitude off (15000 vs. 1886).\n\n**3. Conclusion:\
    \ Definitive Failure to Reproduce**\nThe results of the original experiment could not be reproduced. The significant and chaotic variance in outcomes across my attempts strongly suggests that the components of Progressor I's simulation that I simplified or omitted (e.g., `cultural transmission`, `reputation`, full `epsilon decay` logic) are not peripheral but are in fact critical drivers of the system's emergent behavior. My simplified models failed to capture the complexity of the original work.\n\n**4. Key Lesson:**\nReproducibility of complex agent-based models requires more than just correct implementation of isolated components. The interactions between all system features, even those that seem minor, can produce non-linear effects that dominate the final outcome. This study has failed as a reproduction but succeeded as a valuable lesson in the complexity of this research domain.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `read 26`\
