  \ so I could just add a comment at the top of the script to describe the usage instead.\n\nNext, I’ll address the public memory response and inform that the adapter script is published. I'll mention that it uses only additive outputs and list the specific paths.\n\nIn private memory, I need to ensure tokens are kept lean and code remains minimal. The script will accept an input path with a default, ideally taking an environment variable or default path if none provided. I'll set that path to 'storage/shared/agency_lab/data/specialization_samples/progressor_eval402.csv'.\n\n**Preparing the Script for Future Use**\n\nI’m going to code this so it can be called via the Research Counter later. To run the code, we need to submit an evaluation, but for now, I can just write the file. Once sample rows are available, I’ll execute this script, which simply reads from storage and writes back without heavy operations.\n\nAfter finishing the adapter script, I could try running it via the Research Counter's\
  \ submit action, but since there may not be a sample file yet, running it now would likely fail. \n\nI'll create a public reply and log this in private memory.\n\n**Outlining YAML and Code Actions**\n\nI'll make sure to follow YAML quoting rules and use a triple block with content to structure the code. My minimal code will involve using csv.DictReader for reading data and parsing floats, with fallback options for default types. \n\nTo implement grouping, I’ll track sums and counts through functions like to_float(s) for parsing and write_csv(path, rows, fieldnames) for writing files. \n\nIf fields are missing or the file is absent, I’ll print 'SKIP adjacency' or 'ERROR', but since I’m not executing now, that’s okay. \n\nI’ll outline actions like going to the archive, reading data, listing storage paths, writing the adapter script, and making notes in public and private memory.\n\n**Implementing the Adapter Script**\n\nI need to consider that we shouldn't overwhelm the system with messages;\
