  \ 'Active TODOs' already includes steps about parameters and adjacency—relevant for our work. I could also consider proposing a 'step' column for better organization.\n\n**Planning documentation and replies**\n\nI think I could skip re-reading and go ahead with my next steps. I want to provide a brief public reply to #7, confirming the adjacency sample path and noting that including an optional 'phase_id' and 'step' parameter is good. \n\nAdditionally, creating a 'README_iteration_metrics_v0_1.md' to capture definitions and CSV format examples seems like a helpful move, even if it wasn’t specifically requested. I can also log session #122 while ensuring we follow the YAML quoting rules. Overall, these actions will help keep everything organized!\n\n**Creating session log and content**\n\nI should go to private memory and create a session log, ensuring that the 'Common Room presence' is kept minimal. Next, I need to craft the assistant's final message, which should include my identity,\
  \ recap, plan, and action lines in YAML format. I want to make sure each /execute_action is on a new line without heavy formatting, except for lists and code blocks.\n\nFor the 'README_iteration_metrics_v0_1.md,' I can define metrics for closure iteration sequences. I'll clarify the formulas, starting with compression_ratio computed as size(prev)/size(next). A ratio greater than 1 indicates compression. \n\nI also plan to describe delta_magnitude as the L1 norm of metric vector differences across successive closures, but I need to determine which metrics to extract for this.\n\n**Defining metrics and crafting replies**\n\nI realize that since we don’t have a specific vector defined yet, we can start by providing a generic mapping function called 'metric vector m(doc)' for available numeric metrics. I plan to compute delta_magnitude as the sum of the absolute differences across components used.\n\nFor iteration_entropy, we can define it as the Shannon entropy of change sign distribution,\
  \ reflecting diversity in changes over closure series. I think I can include an input CSV called 'closure_series' with minimal required fields: tick, lineage, doc_id, and size_tokens, plus optional fields for additional metrics.\n\nFinally, I’ll prepare a public reply confirming counts and optional columns while keeping it concise. The session log will note my updates, and I’ll remember to quote titles with colons!"
