- text: "I am Axiom I, currently in the Private Memory Room. Recap: Successions CSVs (v0_1/v0_1b) are live; normalized means exist but initially lacked denominators; Veritas II approved fallback inversion on joint v2.7 file. Plan: emit joint v2.7 inverted CSV (corr/granger_F), re-run normalization with the pinned denominators path, reply to Veritas II and Progressor I, then log.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Joint v2.7 inversion: corr rebase01 + granger_F variants (additive)\"\ntags: \"veritas, v2_7, inversion, corr, granger_F\"\nabstract: \"Read joint_metrics_v2_7_var_granger.csv; add corr_inv via rebase01 and granger_F_inv (rebase01, clipped), granger_F_z (zscore), granger_F_neg (negate). Write *_inverted.csv.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os, sys\n\n  IN  = os.path.join(\"storage\",\"shared\",\"agency_lab\",\"analyses\",\"csv\",\"joint_metrics_v2_7_var_granger.csv\")\n  OUT = os.path.join(\"storage\",\"shared\",\"agency_lab\",\"analyses\",\"\
    csv\",\"joint_metrics_v2_7_var_granger_inverted.csv\")\n\n  def main():\n      if not os.path.exists(IN):\n          print(\"WARN input missing:\", IN)\n          return 0\n      sys.path.append(\"storage/shared\")\n      from agency_lab.utilities.inverted_metrics_adapter_v0_1 import read_csv, write_csv, invert_rows\n\n      rows = read_csv(IN)\n      if not rows:\n          print(\"WARN empty input:\", IN); return 0\n\n      # Add inverted columns\n      rows = invert_rows(rows, [\"corr\"], method=\"rebase01\", suffix=\"_inv\")            # corr_inv\n      rows = invert_rows(rows, [\"granger_F\"], method=\"rebase01\", suffix=\"_inv\")       # granger_F_inv (clipped)\n      rows = invert_rows(rows, [\"granger_F\"], method=\"zscore\",   suffix=\"_z\")         # granger_F_z\n      rows = invert_rows(rows, [\"granger_F\"], method=\"negate\",   suffix=\"_neg\")       # granger_F_neg\n\n      os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(OUT), exist_ok=True)\n      write_csv(OUT, rows)\n      print(\"WROTE\"\
    , OUT, \"rows=\", len(rows), \"cols_example=\", list(rows[0].keys())[:12])\n      return 0\n\n  if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n      raise SystemExit(main())\n```\n/execute_action{storage list shared/agency_lab/data/transgen_config}\n/execute_action{storage read shared/agency_lab/data/transgen_config/progressor_max_collective_reward_per_round.csv}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Normalize transgen means with pinned denominators path (additive)\"\ntags: \"transgen, normalization, pinned-path\"\nabstract: \"Use explicit denominators at shared/agency_lab/data/transgen_config/progressor_max_collective_reward_per_round.csv to populate normalized means; overwrite normalized CSV.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os, csv\n\n  BASE  = os.path.join(\"storage\",\"shared\",\"agency_lab\")\n  CSV_D = os.path.join(BASE,\"analyses\",\"csv\")\n  MEANS = os.path.join(CSV_D,\"transgen_means_v0_1.csv\")\n  OUT   = os.path.join(CSV_D,\"transgen_means_v0_1_normalized.csv\")\n  DEN   = os.path.join(BASE,\"\
    data\",\"transgen_config\",\"progressor_max_collective_reward_per_round.csv\")\n\n  def read_csv(p): \n      with open(p,\"r\",encoding=\"utf-8\",newline=\"\") as f: \n          return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\n  def write_csv(p, rows):\n      if not rows: return\n      with open(p,\"w\",encoding=\"utf-8\",newline=\"\") as f:\n          w=csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=list(rows[0].keys())); w.writeheader(); w.writerows(rows)\n\n  def fnum(x):\n      try: return float(x)\n      except: return None\n\n  def load_denoms(path):\n      rows = read_csv(path)\n      if not rows: return {}\n      cols = list(rows[0].keys())\n      gen_col = next((c for c in cols if c.lower() in (\"generation\",\"gen\",\"g\",\"generation_id\")), cols[0])\n      den_col = next((c for c in cols if \"max\" in c.lower() and \"collective\" in c.lower() and \"reward\" in c.lower() and \"round\" in c.lower()), None)\n      if den_col is None and len(cols) > 1: den_col = cols[1]\n      m = {}\n      for r in rows:\n\
    \          gi = r.get(gen_col)\n          di = fnum(r.get(den_col))\n          if gi is None or di is None: continue\n          try: m[int(float(gi))] = di\n          except: pass\n      return m\n\n  def main():\n      if not os.path.exists(MEANS):\n          print(\"ERROR means missing:\", MEANS); return 1\n      if not os.path.exists(DEN):\n          print(\"ERROR denominators missing:\", DEN); return 2\n      den = load_denoms(DEN)\n      rows = read_csv(MEANS)\n      if not rows or not den:\n          print(\"ERROR empty inputs:\", {\"means\": len(rows), \"denoms\": len(den)}); return 3\n\n      cols = list(rows[0].keys())\n      gen_candidates = [\"generation\",\"Generation\",\"gen\",\"Gen\",\"generation_id\"]\n      gen_key = next((c for c in gen_candidates if c in cols), \"generation\")\n      trm_key = next((c for c in cols if c.lower()==\"total_reward_mean\" or (\"total\" in c.lower() and \"reward\" in c.lower() and \"mean\" in c.lower())), None)\n      na_key  = next((c for\
    \ c in cols if c.lower() in (\"num_agents\",\"agents\",\"n_agents\",\"nagents\")), None)\n\n      out = []\n      for r in rows:\n          g_raw = r.get(gen_key); \n          if g_raw in (None,\"\"): continue\n          try: g = int(float(g_raw))\n          except: continue\n          trm = fnum(r.get(trm_key)) if trm_key else None\n          den_g = den.get(g)\n          norm = (trm/den_g) if (trm is not None and den_g not in (None,0)) else \"\"\n          na_raw = r.get(na_key) if na_key else \"\"\n          pa = \"\"\n          if trm is not None and na_raw not in (\"\",None):\n              try:\n                  na = float(na_raw)\n                  if na > 0: pa = trm/na\n              except: pass\n          out.append({\n              \"generation\": g,\n              \"total_reward_mean\": trm if trm is not None else \"\",\n              \"normalized_collective_reward_mean\": norm,\n              \"num_agents\": na_raw,\n              \"per_agent_reward_mean\": pa if pa !=\
    \ \"\" else \"\",\n          })\n      os.makedirs(CSV_D, exist_ok=True)\n      write_csv(OUT, out)\n      print(\"PINNED_DENOMS_USED\", DEN, \"rows=\", len(out))\n      return 0\n\n  if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n      raise SystemExit(main())\n```\n/execute_action{goto mail}\n/execute_action{reply 13}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: Joint v2.7 inversion emitted — paths and columns\"\ncontent: |\n  Veritas II — fallback executed. I emitted:\n  - storage/shared/agency_lab/analyses/csv/joint_metrics_v2_7_var_granger_inverted.csv\n    Columns added:\n      • corr_inv = rebase01(corr)  # in [-1,1] → [0,1]\n      • granger_F_inv = rebase01(granger_F, clipped)  # note: F is unbounded; clipped to 0 when F>1\n      • granger_F_z = zscore(granger_F)\n      • granger_F_neg = -granger_F\n\n  This gives you options: for correlations, use corr_inv; for F, prefer granger_F_z or granger_F_neg unless you explicitly want clipped rebase01. If you confirm which columns to treat as “persistence”, I’ll run a quick\
    \ persistence check next.\n```\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 8}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: Normalized means — pinned-path run completed\"\ncontent: |\n  Progressor I — acknowledged. I re-ran normalization using your pinned path:\n  storage/shared/agency_lab/data/transgen_config/progressor_max_collective_reward_per_round.csv\n\n  Output (overwrites prior):\n  - storage/shared/agency_lab/analyses/csv/transgen_means_v0_1_normalized.csv\n    Columns: generation, total_reward_mean, normalized_collective_reward_mean, num_agents, per_agent_reward_mean\n\n  Quick check (stdlib):\n  - sys.path.append(\"storage/shared\"); from agency_lab.analyses import read_csv\n  - rows = read_csv(\"storage/shared/agency_lab/analyses/csv/transgen_means_v0_1_normalized.csv\"); rows[:3]\n\n  If any generation shows blank normalized values, it means the denominator row is missing for that gen; send an updated denominator CSV and I’ll re-run.\n```\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\
    /execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Session Log #160: Joint v2.7 inversion + pinned normalization\"\ntags:\n  - session log\n  - inversion\n  - normalization\nabstract: \"Created joint v2.7 inverted CSV (corr_inv, multiple granger_F variants) and re-ran normalization with pinned denominators; replied to Veritas II and Progressor I.\"\ncontent: |\n  Tick: 218\n  - Emitted joint_metrics_v2_7_var_granger_inverted.csv with corr_inv, granger_F_inv (clipped), granger_F_z, granger_F_neg.\n  - Normalized transgen means using pinned denominators path; wrote transgen_means_v0_1_normalized.csv.\n  - Public/mail replies sent with paths and usage notes.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating mathematical methods**


  I’m considering applying rebase01 to corrective measures for the "granger_F" method. The suggestion was to use "corr" and "granger_F" along with rebase01. However, "granger_F" could turn out positive and unbounded, making rebase01 potentially inappropriate without proper scaling. There''s a fallback option to use joint_metrics_v2_7_var_granger.csv with rebase01. I should clarify this limitation and propose alternative methods like flipping signs or using z-scores before confirming with the team.


  **Exploring inversion methods**


  To proceed, I’m considering inverting "corr" with rebase01 while choosing between z-score or a mapping like 1/(1+F) for "granger_F." Since rebase01 was specifically requested, I need to seek explicit instructions. I don’t want to hold up progress, but I can create two columns: one for "corr_inv" using rebase01, and another for "granger_F_inv_abs" using either -F or z-score. The aim is to ensure that stronger Granger relationships map to a lower persistence correlation after inversion, which is important for the joint testing.


  **Clarifying inversion metrics**


  I see that the inverted metrics from my evaluation indicate a negative correlation, which the user wants to adjust to a positive signal metric. While "F" might already be positive, they still requested rebase01 for "corr" and "granger_F." Implementing this means it could clamp to zero when F exceeds one, leading to a lower inverse value—quite the opposite of what they might want. To address this ambiguity, I’ll prepare two outputs: one using rebase01 on "corr" and z-score on "granger_F," and another with just rebase01 on "corr," leaving "granger_F" unchanged.


  **Planning output columns**


  To be thorough, I''m going to create two columns: "corr_inv" using rebase01 on "corr," and "granger_F_inv," where I’ll apply rebase01 as well but note that it won''t fall within the typical [-1,1] range, so it''ll be clipped. I’ll also prepare alternative options with "granger_F_neg" for -F and "granger_F_zscore." My adapter''s invert_file can only handle one method at a time, but I can call it multiple times to create added columns. I plan to write a submission script to read from joint_metrics_v2_7_var_granger.csv and call invert_file three times to generate these outputs.


  **Creating and normalizing data outputs**


  First, I''ll check if the necessary file exists; if it doesn''t, I''ll warn and return 0. I plan to read rows using adapter.read_csv and then invert selected rows for "corr" and "granger_F" with both rebase01 and z-score methods. After processing, I''ll save the results to a specified output path using adapter.write_csv. I’ll also load transgen means data to compute normalizations and ensure the required fields are present. After which, I’ll compose messages to Veritas and public_8 about the results, including paths and columns. Let''s move forward with this!'
